# Business workflow protection

- Never delete any project file or directory. The owner deleted `finish patch`;
  preserve that deletion and do not restore or remove other files. Retain test
  artifacts instead of running filesystem cleanup.

- The owner selected the existing workflows deliberately. Never change business
  rules, operation ordering, state transitions, accounting calculations, stock
  allocation, custody, reconciliation, or approval/recount rules without first
  discussing the concrete change and obtaining the owner's explicit approval.
- A general request to improve, refactor, harden, or align the Dashboard is not
  approval to change business workflows. Preserve previously approved decisions.
- Fix confirmed security, tenant-isolation, and data-integrity defects within the
  authorized scope. If a fix requires a workflow change, explain that dependency
  and obtain approval before applying the workflow change.
- Immediately disclose any accidental workflow change or deletion of important
  files. Do not silently restore or overwrite concurrent user changes.
- Read the actual current files before edits. Use literal verified replacements
  for small edits; multi-file Python patches must validate all baselines and
  anchors before writing any application file. Preserve newer local work.
- InventoryBalance remains the stock source of truth. Every stock mutation goes
  through the Unified Inventory Movement Engine. Preserve FEFO/batches and the
  explicitly chosen DispatchRoute.source_location_id.
- The backend is the final authority. Enforce company_id and PostgreSQL RLS;
  names, client data, localStorage, zones, and branch membership never implicitly
  confer inventory access. Location grants must be explicit.
- Close checkpoints with ESLint, TypeScript, build, meaningful tests, and an
  independent gate. Report actual coverage and remaining limitations; never claim
  complete tenant isolation based only on static checks.
